INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'tntet-working-teachers-2026-20261027', 'global', 'India',
'TNTET 2026 for Working Teachers: Applications Open 27 October, Close 19 November 2026',
'',
'The Teachers Recruitment Board, Tamil Nadu, has opened online applications for the TNTET 2026 for working teachers, from 27 October 2026 to 19 November 2026, with Paper I and Paper II scheduled for 20 December 2026. Only in-service teachers appointed on or before 1 September 2025 can apply.',
'',
$$<p>The Teachers Recruitment Board (TRB), Tamil Nadu, has opened online applications for the Tamil Nadu Teacher Eligibility Test (TNTET) 2026 for working teachers, with the window running from 27 October 2026 to 19 November 2026. The board issued notification number 02/2026 on 26 October 2026, and Paper I and Paper II are scheduled for 20 December 2026. This is the second Special TNTET for in-service teachers this year, following the July session, and it is aimed at one group only: teachers already working in Tamil Nadu schools who were appointed on or before 1 September 2025. It is not an open recruitment drive, and it is not a route for applicants from Nepal or from other Indian states.</p>
<table><tr><th>At a glance: TNTET 2026</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td>Conducting body</td><td>Teachers Recruitment Board (TRB), Tamil Nadu</td></tr><tr><td>Exam</td><td>TNTET 2026 for working teachers</td></tr><tr><td>Notification</td><td>02/2026, issued on 26 October 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Applications open</td><td>27 October 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Applications close</td><td>19 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Correction window</td><td>20 November 2026 to 22 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Exam date</td><td>20 December 2026, Paper I and Paper II</td></tr><tr><td>How to apply</td><td>Online only, through trb.tn.gov.in</td></tr><tr><td>Application fee</td><td>Rs 600 general; Rs 300 SC, SCA, ST and differently abled, per paper</td></tr></table>
<h2>TNTET 2026: who can apply</h2>
<p>The test is only for eligible working teachers. The New Indian Express reported that secondary grade teachers (SGTs), graduate teachers (BT assistants), block resource teacher educators (BRTEs), special education teachers and other equivalent cadre teachers appointed on or before 1 September 2025 can apply. Jagran Josh reported the same cut-off date and cadre list, and said the test covers teachers on time scale, consolidated or part-time pay in government, government-aided and unaided recognised private schools in Tamil Nadu.</p>
<p>That narrow scope is the point of this test. It is a qualifying examination for teachers already in service, not a vacancy announcement, so the notification carries no post count: passing gives eligibility, and any later recruitment would be a separate process.</p>
<h2>Why a second Special TNTET is being held in 2026</h2>
<p>The New Indian Express reported that this is the second Special TET for in-service teachers, following the first examination in July 2026. In July, 220,464 teachers appeared and 108,781 qualified, according to sources in the school education department quoted by the newspaper. The examinations follow a Supreme Court directive requiring teachers appointed before the Right to Education Act to also clear the TET, subject to conditions.</p>
<p>The December session therefore matters most to teachers who did not qualify in July or did not appear then. Applicants should not leave the form to the final days, because the service-verification steps below take time.</p>
<h2>TNTET 2026 papers: Paper I and Paper II</h2>
<p>Paper I is for teachers of Classes 1 to 5, and Paper II is for teachers of Classes 6 to 8. Teachers who teach at both levels may apply for both papers, but Jagran Josh reported that separate applications and separate fees are required for each paper, so applicants should decide which paper or papers match the classes they actually teach before starting the form.</p>
<p>One detail needs care: the reports checked here do not restate the question count, marking scheme or qualifying marks for the December session, so applicants should confirm them in notification 02/2026 itself. The New Indian Express described the 20 December 2026 date as tentative, so watch the board's website for any change.</p>
<h2>TNTET 2026 application fee</h2>
<p>Jagran Josh reported the fee as Rs 600 for general and all other candidates, and Rs 300 for SC, SCA, ST and differently abled candidates, per paper, payable online only through the payment gateway. A teacher applying for both papers therefore pays twice.</p>
<h2>How to apply for TNTET 2026</h2>
<p>Follow these steps, in this order, so the service-verification work is done before the fee is paid:</p>
<ol><li>Read notification 02/2026 in full on <a href="https://trb.tn.gov.in/">trb.tn.gov.in</a>, and confirm your appointment date falls on or before 1 September 2025.</li><li>Get your service certificate verified by the competent authority. The New Indian Express reported that teachers have been asked to get their service registers approved by district and block education officers, and to upload the details on Kalanjiyam, the state government's employee portal, before paying the fee. Do this early, because it depends on other offices.</li><li>On <a href="https://trb.tn.gov.in/">trb.tn.gov.in</a>, open the Online Application Registration Portal and select Apply for Paper I or Paper II, as applicable.</li><li>Enter your email address and mobile number, then submit to register.</li><li>Fill in the form accurately, and upload the scanned documents in the prescribed format, including the verified service certificate.</li><li>Pay the fee online through the payment gateway, submit the form, and save a copy of the submitted application.</li><li>If you spot an error after submission, use the correction window from 20 November 2026 to 22 November 2026, and check that the form shows as submitted, not merely saved.</li></ol>
<h2>What to check before you pay</h2>
<p>First, check the appointment cut-off: a teacher appointed after 1 September 2025 is outside this notification, and paying the fee does not change that. Second, check the paper, since each paper needs its own application and payment. Third, apply only through <a href="https://trb.tn.gov.in/">trb.tn.gov.in</a>. No agent can secure a place on this test, and nobody asking for money beyond the published fee, or promising a qualifying result, acts for the board.</p>
<p>For readers in Nepal, the position is plain: this test is for teachers working in Tamil Nadu schools under the board's rules. It is not a teaching job abroad, and it does not open a route into Indian government teaching for applicants based in Nepal.</p>
<p>Also on Growentix: <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/bpsc-tre-4-teacher-recruitment-33320-posts-20261025">BPSC TRE 4.0 teacher recruitment</a> in Bihar and <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/oavs-principals-teachers-815-posts-20261025">OAVS principals and teachers recruitment</a> in Odisha show how other states run separate teacher processes.</p>
<p>Sources: the Teachers Recruitment Board, Tamil Nadu, notification 02/2026, via <a href="https://trb.tn.gov.in/">trb.tn.gov.in</a>; The New Indian Express, which reported the notification, the July figures and the service-verification steps; and Jagran Josh, which reported the dates, fee and steps. Where any report differs from the notification, the notification is the final word.</p>$$,
